        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "8699b447-f09b-5d54-8099-0565c8634ac1",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Alberta, Canada, United Kingdom\n- Systems: Constitution of Alberta, Westminster system, parliamentary procedure\n- Organizations: deliberative assembly\n- Roles: Clerk (legislature)\n- Concepts: laying before the house, unwritten constitution, oath of office\n\nFacts:\n- The Constitution of Alberta describes the fundamental rules under which the Canadian province of Alberta is governed.\n- Alberta has an unwritten constitution, which is typical of Canadian provinces and Westminster systems.\n- The Westminster system is a parliamentary system of government modelled after the system that developed in the United Kingdom.\n- In parliamentary procedure, especially in the Westminster system, a document is laid before the house when it is formally recognised as having been made available for members of a deliberative assembly to read.\n- A Clerk in a legislature may keep custody of documents laid before the house, received, or produced; make records of proceedings; allocate office space; enrol members; and administer an oath of office.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
